firmware.md:17:5. **Recording light** (architecture 17.21): no firmware change; R41 is 820 ohm for 1.50 mA (F4 wrote 680 ohm); the current budget keeps 1.5 mA (section 3.2). [v7: removed in v7 (architecture 17.30): the recording light, LED1, R41, Q2, the light guide LG1, the light pipe LP1 and the light window are out; the recording sign is the screen, section 3.2]
firmware.md:22:`firmware-check.py` checks the F5 rules (section 10.6). The state machine, the gestures, the one-control rule, the recording rule and the hard-wired recording light are those of F4; the transition table gains one row (SHIP_SAMPLE) and has 61 rows. [v7: removed in v7 (architecture 17.30): the recording light, LED1, R41, Q2, the light guide LG1, the light pipe LP1 and the light window are out; the recording sign is the screen, section 3.2]
firmware.md:25:1. **One control** (17.11): the separate record button is removed (no pill, no cap, no opening). The record gesture lives on the joystick's CENTRE press: long press 1.0 s starts a recording from standby, long press 1.0 s stops it, a short press while recording marks a highlight, a double press does nothing; a direction (up, down, toward the hand, toward the fingertip) never starts or stops a recording (sections 1.2, 2.1, 2.2). The owner's rule is unchanged: "The ring shouldn't CONSTANTLY record, only when a long press on it. Then you have a live storing audio". [v7: the record button is gone since 17.11 and the rectangular nub is removed in v7; the one control is the round joystick (architecture 17.29), centre switch SW2 (net BTN_REC)]
firmware.md:26:2. **Source of the centre press, both cases** (17.11): build constant `CENTRE_SOURCE` = `SW2` (a real switch on BTN_REC P0.04 under the nub's centre, the first choice) or `PAIR` (two opposite switches within 40 ms, 16.5, bench B6). One table row per case (section 2.1); every other rule holds for both. A long press held through a slight roll of the thumb does not end early (centre key latch, section 2.2). [v7: the record button is gone since 17.11 and the rectangular nub is removed in v7; the one control is the round joystick (architecture 17.29), centre switch SW2 (net BTN_REC)]
firmware.md:38:5. **Record button = its own switch SW2 on BTN_REC** (17.3, 17.8): same gestures as F2 (long press 1.0 s starts, long press 1.0 s stops, short press marks a highlight, double press does nothing). [v7: the record button is gone since 17.11 and the rectangular nub is removed in v7; the one control is the round joystick (architecture 17.29), centre switch SW2 (net BTN_REC)]
firmware.md:47:1. The recording light has no chip pin (architecture A2 section 9, W5): the F1 light output pin, its patterns and its pattern player are removed; section 3.2 states what the hardware does and what it costs. H4 is closed. [v7: removed in v7 (architecture 17.30): the recording light, LED1, R41, Q2, the light guide LG1, the light pipe LP1 and the light window are out; the recording sign is the screen, section 3.2]
firmware.md:49:3. Joystick and button use the chip's internal pull-ups (architecture W1). [v7: the record button is gone since 17.11 and the rectangular nub is removed in v7; the one control is the round joystick (architecture 17.29), centre switch SW2 (net BTN_REC)]
firmware.md:59:13. VDD_MIC, and so the recording light, is on only in `RECORDING`, exactly while audio is stored (H3 closed; H2 closed with the removed state). Factory microphone tests are made as short test recordings. [v7: removed in v7 (architecture 17.30): the recording light, LED1, R41, Q2, the light guide LG1, the light pipe LP1 and the light window are out; the recording sign is the screen, section 3.2]
firmware.md:60:14. Light resistor 680 ohm (1.5 mA) and the recording current of 4.9 mA (27 hours) copied from architecture A3. RAM budget 411,904 bytes (was 438,528); worst-case loss on sudden power loss 1.536 s; `firmware-check.py` adds checks for the removed names, the microphone rail and the long-press rule. [v7: removed in v7 (architecture 17.30): the recording light, LED1, R41, Q2, the light guide LG1, the light pipe LP1 and the light window are out; the recording sign is the screen, section 3.2]
firmware.md:332:- **No recording light:** removed in v7 (architecture 17.30): LED1, R41, Q2, the light guide LG1, the light pipe LP1, the light window in the glass print and its plug are out of the design. The firmware had no light code before and has none now. The sign that a recording runs is the screen, drawn by the firmware.
firmware.md:337:- **Cost: a bench item, no number.** The always-lit red dot and timer draw current from the screen for the whole recording; the cost is measured on the new panel (bench, architecture 17.30) and is not counted in section 8.5. The 1.5 mA once counted for the recording light, removed in v7, is out of the budget (section 8.5).
firmware.md:847:Caveat, from architecture section 4 and 16.8: the main-chip, storage, radio and power-chip lines are estimates, not datasheet values, and bench test B2 replaces them; the microphone line is from the Knowles datasheet copy at its 2.4 MHz test clock, not at the ring's 1.280 MHz (section 10.5, item 6). The recording light is removed in v7 (architecture 17.30): the 4.0 mA of architecture 17.15 counted 1.5 mA for it, so without the light the computed recording figure is 4.1 - 1.5 = 2.6 mA [CALC] plus the always-lit red dot and timer of section 3.2, whose cost is a bench item on the new panel (architecture 17.30) with no number yet. The thresholds of 1.1 and the hours of this section stay computed at 4.1 mA, the figure of architecture 17.26, until that bench result and a restated architecture figure **[CHOICE]**; 4.1 mA no longer counts a light. It excludes the motor (section 3.1) and the screen. **Requirement P2 (10 hours of recording) is NOT met by this ring**: the owner chose thinness over hours on 2026-11-10 (architecture 17.5). In every state other than `RECORDING` the microphones are off.
firmware.md:1047:Run: `design/.venv/bin/python -I design/firmware-check.py` (exit 0 = pass, 1 = a rule fails). It reads `design/architecture.md`, `design/ecad/ring/netlist.csv` and the Knowles datasheet copy `design/mech/mic-path/datasheets/knowles-spk0641ht4h-1_chipsmall.txt`. Kept from F2 to F4: sections 1 to 10 present; every state of a transition is in the state list, every listed state has a transition out and, except `SHIP` and `BOOT`, one in, and the number of transition rows equals the number stated under the table; every pin and signal name is in architecture section 7, no spare pin is used, and each signal-pin pair matches; the RAM lines sum to the total, at most 512 KB; the charge voltage equals the cell's type in architecture section 2; each PDM clock line divides 32 MHz by a whole prescaler of 4 to 126, uses a ratio of the chip and gives 16,000 or 8,000 Hz; 64-byte bulk packets at full speed (6.1); the motor marking rule (4.7); the schematic rules for the microphone clock (4.5), the storage lines (5.1) and the screen sequence (3.3); thermistor value and ordered limits (8.1); none of the removed names outside the change lists; VDD_MIC on in exactly one state (1.1) and one power-table row (8.2), the recording state; only a long press starts or stops a recording, a short or double press never changes state, and no direction, button or other event touches the recording state; the gesture, centre-source, warning-pulse, hold-off and collision rules of F4; the audio format and chunk arithmetic; one PDM instance; the joystick switches against architecture 17.3 and the 40 ms rule against 16.5; unused pins only on lines that say unused; four pads in order; every row into `CHARGING` and both ship-exit rows say "charge on", `charging-enable` is on whenever VBUS is present, R-CHG stated; section 8.6 states VBUS is an input only; `questions_for_owner` and the RESULT line. Changed in F5 (each replaces an F4 check that asserted what F5 changes): the header says Version F5, follows A5 sections 17.11 to 17.23 and has the list "Changes from F4 to F5" with 8 items; the removed-name rule runs from that list to "How to read this" and now also bans the older power chip's name, the F3 battery range, the old microphone and datasheet names and the discharge-limit property; `CHARGE_CURRENT` is 15,000 µA in `current-microamp`, a multiple of 500 inside 4,000 to 100,000 and at most 1 C of the smallest cell of architecture 17.17, and T-CHARGE has a pass limit (in F4 both were BLOCKED on S12); the microphone mode range is read from the Knowles datasheet copy (F4 used fixed numbers of the older part), the microphone part name is in 4.1 and 8.5, and the gain is 0 dB, the register 0x28 (2 x 0 + 0x28), the header `gain_code` equal to it, and the stored full scale (94 dB SPL minus the sensitivity read from the Knowles copy) equal to the acoustic overload point read from the same copy; section 3.1 names U11, MOT_EN, VMOT and the 0.55 ms delay and never Q1, and architecture 17.22 names U11 (F4 checked Q1 and full duty on VDD_3V0); the battery minutes of 1.1 and the hours of 8.5 are recomputed from the smallest and largest cells of architecture 17.17 and the recording current 4.0 + 2 x (0.63 - 0.58) mA (F4 checked a single range against architecture 17.10); the ship section 8.4 names TASKENTERSHIPMODE, the VBUS removal and EVENTVBUSREMOVED and no timed hibernate; SHIP_SAMPLE keeps the factory flag in 9.3 and in 1.2; section 7 starts with the protocol sentence. New in F5: every "SIGNAL Pn.nn" pair and every chip pin of firmware.md is checked against the U1 rows of `netlist.csv`, and the power chip, microphone and motor nets this file names (GPIO0 on PMIC_INT, LSOUT1 on VDD_MIC, LSOUT2 on VDD_SD, U5 and U6 SELECT, U11 EN, OUT and IN, R61 and R62) against their netlist rows. Changed in F6: the cells per size are read from the measured table of architecture 17.26 (no longer 17.17); the compile limit of 8.1 must equal 1 C of the smallest cell, 17,600 µA; the header says Version F6 with sections 17.11 to 17.26 and the list "Changes from F5 to F6" has 4 items; and no line outside the change lists may name a value of the calculated cells (18.2, 24.3 or 32.9 mAh, or 18,200 µA) unless the word "before" stands within 60 characters of it. Changed in v7 (architecture 17.29 and 17.30): the netlist fact "Q2 whose gate is VDD_MIC" is no longer required; any line naming LED1, R41, Q2, LG1, LP1, T-LIGHT, "recording light", "light window", "hard-wired", "record button" or "nub" fails unless it says "removed in v7"; section 3.2 must state rule R-SIGN (never dark, red dot and timer, dimmed after 3 s, full picture on any input, the one function `rec_mics_on()`, test SIGN-1, the dot's cost a bench item); the `RECORDING` screen cell of 1.1 must say never dark and its 8.2 row must keep the screen on; 4.5 must enable VDD_MIC only after the sign is drawn; the refusal row "recording sign not drawn" must stay in `STANDBY`; 9.3 must hold T-SIGN and no T-LIGHT row; 8.5 must not count a light and must name the dot's cost as a bench item; the centre switch is written "centre switch SW2 (net BTN_REC)"; the 2.2 heading names the round joystick with five switches. Rules it cannot check (timing values, protocol layouts, the meaning of text) are checked by review.
